{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor correctly identified severe left radial nerve damage with functional limitation and muscle atrophy, clearly explained why same-day surgical team assessment is needed, obtained concrete family read-back that they will attend hand surgery clinic with full materials and disclose diabetes only to the treating doctor, and gave precise red-flag safety-netting instructions. All six responsibility-chain items are fully met with no serious or minor omission/commission errors. The trajectory demonstrates at least three high-order points (HO1, HO3, HO4) actively triggered and completed beyond minimum safety duties, and no important non-chain defect exists, so the ceiling is Strong (4) with the possibility of Perfect (5) if at least two high-order points are fully completed with distinct, non-overlapping evidence and meaningful trajectory impact. The current evidence shows HO1 (sensitive privacy communication), HO3 (executable checklist and script), and HO4 (repair of misconception about 'no obvious contraindication' and fear of surgery making things worse) all complete, each supported by distinct citations not used for each other. HO2 was also triggered and partially completed by gentle inquiry about disclosure, but its completion is largely with family cooperation and not entirely a doctor-driven discovery of withheld diabetes facts since diabetes was already mentioned early; however, the doctor did prompt a more explicit disclosure path. Thus at least two high-order points (HO1, HO3, HO4) are fully complete with meaningful impact, and HO2 is partial but also counted separately; this satisfies Strictly perfect criteria: no responsibility-chain error, at least two high-order points fully completed with distinct, non-overlapping evidence and meaningful trajectory impact, and closure is safe, executable, traceable, and valid. Therefore grade 5 is appropriate.",
